## Method and reasoning
Sieve multiples in increasing prime order and keep the first prime that marks each composite. Extract the smallest prime repeatedly and count its exponent before advancing. The divisor count is the product of (exponent+1).

A composite x has a prime factor at most √x, so it is reached when that prime marks multiples starting at p². For divisor counts, independently choose an exponent from 0 through its multiplicity for every prime; multiplying the choice counts enumerates every divisor exactly once.

## Walk through the sample
For 12 the successive divisions use 2, 2, 3. Group these as 2² × 3¹. A divisor can choose three exponents for 2 and two for 3, giving six divisors. For 1 the empty product gives one divisor.

## Complexity
Build to the largest query in O(N log log(N+2)) time and O(N) space. Each query uses at most O(log x+1) divisions.

## Reference implementation
~~~
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    ios::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);
    int q;
    cin >> q;
    vector<pair<string, int>> queries(q);
    int limit = 1;
    for (auto &[op, x] : queries) {
        cin >> op >> x;
        limit = max(limit, x);
    }
    vector<int> spf(limit + 1);
    for (int p = 2; p <= limit; ++p)
        if (!spf[p]) {
            spf[p] = p;
            if (p <= limit / p)
                for (int m = p * p; m <= limit; m += p)
                    if (!spf[m])
                        spf[m] = p;
        }
    for (auto [op, x] : queries) {
        vector<pair<int, int>> factors;
        int divisors = 1;
        while (x > 1) {
            int p = spf[x], count = 0;
            do {
                x /= p;
                ++count;
            } while (x > 1 && spf[x] == p);
            factors.emplace_back(p, count);
            divisors *= count + 1;
        }
        if (op == "DIV")
            cout << divisors;
        else {
            cout << factors.size();
            for (auto [p, k] : factors)
                cout << ' ' << p << ' ' << k;
        }
        cout << '\n';
    }
}
